1. Install required software
sudo apt update && sudo apt install nodejs npm openjdk-17-jdk maven -y

Description: Installs Node.js, npm, Java 17 and Maven.

Expected output:

Setting up nodejs ...
Setting up npm ...
Setting up openjdk-17-jdk ...
Setting up maven ...
2. Verify installations
node -v

Description: Checks the Node.js version.

Output:

v18.x.x

Then:

java -version

Description: Checks the Java version.

Output:

openjdk version "17.x.x"

And:

mvn -version

Description: Checks the Maven installation.

Output:

Apache Maven 3.x.x
Java version: 17.x.x
3. Create JavaScript application
mkdir -p ~/karate-js && cd ~/karate-js

Description: Creates and opens the JavaScript project folder.

Output:

No output
4. Initialize Node.js project
npm init -y

Description: Creates the Node.js package.json file.

Output:

Wrote to /home/ubuntu/karate-js/package.json
5. Install Express
npm install express

Description: Installs Express to create the REST API.

Output:

added xx packages
6. Create app.js

You can use nano:

nano app.js

Description: Opens a file to create the JavaScript application.

Paste:

const express = require('express');

const app = express();

app.get('/hello', (req, res) => {
    res.json({ message: "Hello from JavaScript!" });
});

app.listen(3000, () => {
    console.log("Server running on port 3000");
});

Save:

Ctrl + O → Enter → Ctrl + X

7. Run JavaScript application 
node app.js

Description: Starts the Node.js REST API server.

Output:

Server running on port 3000
Keep this terminal running.

8. Test the JavaScript API

Open a second terminal.

curl http://localhost:3000/hello

Description: Sends a GET request to the JavaScript REST API.

Output:

{"message":"Hello from JavaScript!"}
This confirms your JavaScript API works.

9. Create Karate testing project

In the second terminal:

cd ~/karate-js
mkdir -p karate-test/src/test/java
cd karate-test

Description: Creates the Maven project structure for Karate testing.

Output:

No output
10. Create pom.xml
nano pom.xml

Description: Creates the Maven configuration file for Karate.

Paste:

<project xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0">

    <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>

    <groupId>com.example</groupId>
    <artifactId>karate-test</artifactId>
    <version>1.0</version>

    <properties>
        <maven.compiler.source>17</maven.compiler.source>
        <maven.compiler.target>17</maven.compiler.target>
    </properties>

    <dependencies>
        <dependency>
            <groupId>com.intuit.karate</groupId>
            <artifactId>karate-junit5</artifactId>
            <version>1.4.1</version>
            <scope>test</scope>
        </dependency>
    </dependencies>

</project>

Save:

Ctrl + O → Enter → Ctrl + X

11. Create Karate feature
nano src/test/java/hello.feature

Description: Creates the Karate test scenario.

Paste:

Feature: JavaScript API Testing

Scenario: Test Hello API
    Given url 'http://localhost:3000/hello'
    When method GET
    Then status 200
    And match response.message == 'Hello from JavaScript!'

Save:

Ctrl + O → Enter → Ctrl + X

12. Create KarateTest.java
nano src/test/java/KarateTest.java

Description: Creates the Java runner for the Karate test.

Paste:

import com.intuit.karate.junit5.Karate;

class KarateTest {

    @Karate.Test
    Karate testHello() {
        return Karate.run("hello").relativeTo(getClass());
    }
}

Save:

Ctrl + O → Enter → Ctrl + X

13. Run automated test 

Make sure Terminal 1 still has Node.js running:

Server running on port 3000

In Terminal 2, make sure you are here:

~/karate-js/karate-test

Then run:

mvn test

Description: Runs the Karate automated API test.

Expected output:
Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0, Skipped: 0

Finally:

BUILD SUCCESS
Your test passed.
